{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat .agents/skills/pose-review/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: pose-review\ndescription: Use for POSE pull-request or code review to verify controlled scope, preserved contracts, security and observability impact, risk-proportional validation, and escalation where applicable. Trigger keywords - review, code review, PR review, review opinion, ultrareview.\nwhen_to_use: Evaluating your own or another author's diff or PR under POSE. Use before commenting or approving to select rules, inspect validation evidence and prior decisions, and issue an actionable decision.\npose_schema_range: \"1-1\"\nclients: agents-skills, mcp, claude-code\ncapabilities: read\n---\n\n# Skill: pose-review\n\n## Required reading\n\n1. [AGENTS.md](../../../AGENTS.md).\n2. [`.pose/workflows/review.md`](../../../.pose/workflows/review.md).\n3. Applicable domain rules; security takes precedence in conflicts.\n\nResolve the selected task with `pose context --task <typed-or-qualified-artifact-ref> --json` before reviewing. Follow its canonical authority and stop on ambiguity, stale binding, unsupported metadata or `transfer-in-progress`. Use the qualified `xref:` scope for cross-project review; every cross-project write requires an explicit `POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS` binding and the current `context_revision` as `--expect-context`. Keep review evidence and its `POSE-Spec:` trailer in the authority repository.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Classify the change as feature, bugfix, refactor, documentation, or mixed.\n2. Run `pose assess tech-debt` during code review to ensure markers (`TODO`, `FIXME`, `stub`, `panic`) are covered by follow-ups or specs. Run `pose assess integrate` when touching inter-module contracts (Protobuf, Kafka, REST, MCP).\n3. Resolve `pose review bundle <scope> --explain` to inspect effective criteria, components and tool requirements.\n4. Run active required tools first, record why each recommended tool was used or skipped, and keep completion tools deferred until attestation.\n5. Select rules with `pose suggest review --path <affected-dir>` for every mapped component.\n6. Search `.pose/knowledge/` for prior module decisions, accepted risks, and pending follow-ups.\n7. Require deterministic `pose validate --strict` evidence matching delivery targets.\n8. Evaluate every required plan criterion, including cross-component boundaries. When the change adds or alters a governance gate, also answer: what is the cheapest way to satisfy this gate in form without producing its value, and what stops that? Point to the adversarial corpus case that proves it (`pose-mcp/internal/cli/testdata/adversarial/README.md`), or record the gap.\n9. Classify findings as critical, high, medium, or low with evidence and expected action.\n10. Run `pose recurrence-check --tolerant --window-days 14`; use recurrence escalation for a matching systemic signal.\n11. Create a handoff with `pose new-knowledge handoff <slug>` for accepted residual risk, monitoring, or deferred action.\n12. Seal the review subject with `pose review bundle <scope> --seal`.\n13. Prepare the mechanical half with `pose review auto-attest <bundle-id> --reviewer agent:<id>` — without `--apply` — and read `review_attestation.pending`: those are the criteria no registered check answers, and they are yours to answer. Then record the attestation with `pose review attest`, giving every judgment criterion a conclusion. `--apply` on auto-attest records only when nothing is pending; it refuses otherwise, and never answers a judgment criterion for you. An explicit attestation records `--criterion ID|passed|evidence` (or `not-applicable` with a rationale, or `finding` naming a recorded finding) and `--finding ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by]`; every finding needs a severity and an action, and an `accepted-risk` or `wont-fix` finding also needs an owner, a rationale and a review date. A `passed` criterion may cite only evidence the sealed bundle contains, of a class the criterion accepts. For a cross-project attestation, use the qualified `xref:` as the target argument to `pose review attest`, include `--expect-context <digest>` with `--apply`, and keep the explicit project binding; a bundle ID alone does not select an authority project.\n14. Run `pose review verify <scope>` and `pose review-check <scope>`; close only when both confirm a valid, approved attestation.\n15. Decide: approved, approved with reservations, changes requested, or rejected.\n16. When Contributor Mode is active, if review uncovers POSE linter false-positives, diagnostic frictions, or rule gaps, stage an improvement proposal with `pose contribute stage --type enhancement --title \"<summary>\"`.\n\n## Output requirements\n\n- Completed Rules applied during review section.\n- Bundle/plan digests and dispositions for required, recommended and deferred completion tools.\n- Severity-classified findings with expected actions.\n- Clear and actionable final decision.\n- Handoff when residual risk is accepted.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'pwd; git status --short; command -v pose; cat .pose/specs/pose-federated-proof-reads-each-input-once.md; cat .pose/workflows/review.md; cat .pose/rules/security.md; cat .pose/rules/delivery-evidence.md; rg --files -g AGENTS.md pose-mcp; git diff bf2ff02..8cb6cf3 --stat'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'pwd; git status --short; command -v pose; cat .pose/specs/pose-federated-proof-reads-each-input-once.md; cat .pose/workflows/review.md; cat .pose/rules/security.md; cat .pose/rules/delivery-evidence.md; rg --files -g AGENTS.md pose-mcp; git diff bf2ff02..8cb6cf3 --stat'","aggregated_output":"/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-1821732801/worktree\n/home/go/.local/bin/pose\ncat: .pose/specs/pose-federated-proof-reads-each-input-once.md: No such file or directory\n# Workflow: Review\n\n## Objective\n\nVerify that a change is correct, production-safe, and aligned with its approved scope and specs.\n\n## Preconditions\n\n- Make the final diff readable through cohesive commits or batches.\n- Provide requirement and spec context.\n- Attach implementer validation evidence, including `pose validate` output.\n- Define acceptance criteria and expected risk.\n\n## Execution checklist\n\n1. Resolve `pose review bundle <scope> --explain` (or `pose_review_bundle`) when bundle policy is enabled; otherwise resolve `pose review-plan <scope> --explain`.\n2. Confirm the semantic scope, patch/tree identity, mapped components and bundle plan digest.\n3. Run required active tools first, evaluate recommended tools explicitly and leave completion-only tools deferred until an attestation exists.\n4. Select the plan's applicable rules explicitly and record them in the review.\n5. Resolve rule conflicts with the most restrictive option, prioritizing security for exposure, authorization, and integrity risks.\n6. Consult `.pose/knowledge/` for relevant handoffs, accepted risks, follow-ups, and decision logs.\n7. Check compliance with specs, contracts, and local instructions.\n8. Review functional correctness and edge cases.\n9. Evaluate security, observability, and performance risks.\n10. Require validation evidence from `.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json` proportional to risk.\n11. Identify regression and rollout or rollback risks.\n12. Run plan-selected assessments, including tech-debt and integration when applicable.\n13. Classify findings by severity and propose objective actions.\n14. **Produce a handoff** in `.pose/knowledge/` when findings result in accepted risk, post-merge monitoring, or deferred work (`pose new-knowledge handoff <slug>`); link it from the review.\n15. Seal the validated subject with `pose review bundle <scope> --seal`; stop if any input is unclassified or changed since preparation.\n16. Issue a final decision: approved, approved with reservations, changes requested, or rejected.\n17. Record the decision with `pose review attest <bundle-id> ... --plan-digest <sha256> --apply`; signed external envelopes use `pose review attest --envelope <path> --apply`.\n18. Run `pose review verify <scope>` and `pose review-check <scope>`; reject stale, incomplete, conflicting or transitively invalid reused criteria.\n19. Apply closeout only after verification reports `ready-to-close`; lifecycle, state and report refreshes must not change the sealed bundle.\n20. When Contributor Mode is active and review identifies linter false-positives, diagnostic frictions, or POSE rule gaps, stage an improvement proposal via `pose contribute stage --type enhancement`.\n\n## Required rule selection\n\nAttach this section to the review:\n\n```md\n## Rules applied during review\n- Change type: <feature|bugfix|refactor|documentation-update|mixed>\n- Workflow consulted: `.pose/workflows/<file>.md`\n- Rules selected:\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/security.md`\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/backend-go.md` (extension `pose-rule-backend-go`, when installed)\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/frontend-react.md` (extension `pose-rule-frontend-react`, when installed)\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/kubernetes.md` (extension `pose-rule-kubernetes`, when installed)\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/documentation-style.md`\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/knowledge-governance.md` when knowledge or process changes\n- Rationale for each selected rule: <one line per item>\n- Rules not applicable: <list and explain>\n```\n\n## Recurrence escalation\n\n- Trigger `.pose/workflows/recurrence-escalation.md` when recurring rework is uncovered and the threshold is met.\n- Require `recurrence_rework_uncovered` evidence for the 30-day period.\n- Require explicit links from the specialized workflow to applicable domain rules.\n- Require a formal keep, adjust, or discard decision after the 45-day pilot.\n\n## Minimum domain coverage\n\n- React UI: apply `frontend-react`, `security`, and `documentation-style`.\n- Go services: apply `backend-go`, `security`, and `documentation-style`.\n- Cluster deployment or infrastructure: apply `kubernetes`, `security`, and `documentation-style`.\n- Process, spec, workflow, rule, or report: apply `documentation-style`, `knowledge-governance`, and security when sensitive data is involved.\n- Cross-stack changes: apply every touched domain rule cumulatively.\n\n## Domain review checklist\n\n### Security\n\n- Confirm authentication, authorization, and least privilege when applicable.\n- Verify that code, configuration, manifests, docs, and logs contain no secrets.\n- Require applicable vulnerability and secret-scanning evidence.\n\n### Contracts\n\n- Confirm compatibility of public HTTP, event, schema, CLI, and file contracts.\n- Validate forward and backward compatibility for rollout and rollback.\n- Require a spec update for contract changes.\n\n### Observability\n\n- Verify structured logs and metrics without sensitive data.\n- Confirm that probes, health checks, and alerts reflect real behavior.\n- Preserve enough traceability for post-deployment diagnosis.\n\n### Validation\n\n- Require lint, typecheck, test, and build coverage proportional to risk.\n- Require executed `pose validate` evidence and relevant results.\n- Record environment limitations and residual validation risks.\n\n## Editorial checklist\n\n- Use imperative, actionable instructions.\n- Keep bullets short and avoid duplicated sections.\n- Use `check`, `spec`, and `workflow` consistently.\n- Reference explicit files and paths.\n\n## Required outputs\n\n- A review decision with rationale.\n- The inspected effective plan, its component provenance and tool dispositions.\n- An immutable sealed review bundle and separate attestation bound to its exact digest.\n- A completed rule-selection section.\n- Findings with severity, evidence, and recommendation.\n- Recurrence classification by domain and cause with preventive actions.\n- An explicit statement about public contracts and compatibility.\n- Residual risks and safe-deployment conditions.\n- References to executed checks and collected evidence.\n\n## Complete multi-rule review example\n\n```md\n## Review Summary\n- Decision: approved with reservations\n- Change type: feature (Go API, React UI, and Helm)\n- Workflow: `.pose/workflows/feature.md`\n\n## Rules applied during review\n- `.pose/rules/backend-go.md` (extension `pose-rule-backend-go`): verified handlers, context, and error handling.\n- `.pose/rules/frontend-react.md` (extension `pose-rule-frontend-react`): verified accessibility and explicit loading and error states.\n- `.pose/rules/kubernetes.md` (extension `pose-rule-kubernetes`): verified resources, probes, and immutable images.\n- `.pose/rules/security.md`: verified authorization and absence of secrets.\n- `.pose/rules/documentation-style.md`: verified editorial consistency.\n\n## Checks and evidence\n- `pose validate`: passed\n- `go test ./...`: passed in the backend module\n- `pnpm lint && pnpm test`: passed in the frontend module\n- `helm template` and `kubectl apply --dry-run=client`: passed\n\n## Contracts and compatibility\n- Preserved `POST /v1/storage`.\n- Added optional `retentionDays`, preserving backward compatibility.\n\n## Findings\n- Medium: missing queue-saturation alert; add a metric and alert before production.\n- Low: frontend error lacks request-id context; improve observable UX.\n\n## Residual risks\n- Review did not simulate real cluster load.\n- Increase monitoring during the first 24 hours.\n```\n\n## CI failure interpretation\n\n- Treat `POSE check (strict)` as merge-blocking on protected branches.\n- Treat failed required checks in `POSE validate (strict)` as merge-blocking.\n- Attach pipeline artifacts: `pose-check.log`, `pose-validate.latest.log`, and the POSE report.\n- Classify optional-check failures as quality reservations with an owned remediation plan.\n- Reject changes that violate specs, public contracts, security, or safe rollout.\n\n## Phased rollout for unready modules\n\n- Enforce the current required checks immediately.\n- Use `moduleOverrides` to phase adoption without weakening global structural and required gates.\n- Promote optional checks to required by module with an owner and agreed window.\n\n### Optional-to-required promotion protocol\n\n- Select a pilot domain and map candidate checks with owners and explicit risks.\n- Measure success for four weeks and require a baseline of at least 95 percent.\n- Promote only eligible domains through `moduleOverrides`.\n- Update the validation matrix and quality policy in the same change set.\n- Monitor regressions and adjust rollout without removing established required gates.\n- Update specs and rules when rollout changes merge acceptance criteria.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- Resolve or formally accept every critical and high finding.\n- Make the final decision clear and actionable.\n- Support quality and security conclusions with evidence.\n- Preserve approved scope without unjustified drift.\n\n## Reviewer mode\n\n**Objective:** evaluate technical quality and production readiness with emphasis on correctness, risk, and scope.\n\n- **Focus:** functional correctness, spec consistency, regression and security risk, operability, validation quality, and actionable feedback.\n- **Anti-patterns:** approve without sufficient evidence; review style while ignoring functional risk; request unrelated changes; block progress on subjective preference.\n- **Handoff:** state the decision, severity and evidence for each finding, safe merge or deployment conditions, accepted risks, and recommended monitoring.\n# Rule: Security\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide for authentication, authorization, sensitive data, external integrations, dependencies, and attack surfaces.\n\n## Required patterns\n\n- Apply least privilege to resource and credential access.\n- Store secrets only in appropriate secret-management mechanisms, never in code.\n- Validate and sanitize external input for its execution context.\n- Keep personal and confidential data out of plaintext logs and metrics.\n- Evaluate new dependencies for maintenance, licensing, and known vulnerabilities.\n- Test both positive and negative authentication and authorization cases.\n\n## Blocking anti-patterns\n\n- Committing credentials, tokens, keys, or secrets in any versioned artifact.\n- Disabling TLS or certificate verification without a formally documented mitigation.\n- Trusting client input for authorization decisions.\n- Executing dynamic commands without adequate validation and escaping.\n- Ignoring critical security alerts without an approved exception record.\n\n## Minimum checks\n\n- Run secret scanning on the changed diff or scope.\n- Run the module's applicable dependency vulnerability scanner.\n- Pass the relevant authentication and authorization tests.\n- Complete a review for sensitive-data exposure in logs and configuration.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Apply the most restrictive security, contract, and operational rule when domain rules conflict.\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence and explicit risk mitigation when speed conflicts with control.\n- Record the precedence decision and objective rationale in the review.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\n# Rule: Delivery Evidence\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide when writing or reviewing any document that **claims delivery,\ncompletion, or readiness**: status reports, handoffs, summaries, module READMEs,\ncurrent-state sections in specs or proposals, and messages that say something is complete.\n\n## Required conventions\n\n- Claim delivery only with attached, verifiable gate evidence: command and output\n  (`pose validate`, `go test`, `tsc`, `vitest`) or a link to the corresponding POSE report.\n- Use POSE lifecycle vocabulary: `draft`, `in-progress`, `done`, `blocked`,\n  `superseded`, or `abandoned`. Do not invent labels such as `completed` or `100% COMPLETE`.\n- Separate **implemented and verified** work from **planned or documented** work.\n  A plan describes intent; do not present that intent as current reality.\n- Reference the report or evidence that crossed the spec exit gate before using `done`.\n- Convert relative dates to absolute dates and stamp the verification date.\n- Declare exact source-tree artifact actions in the spec and reconcile them\n  against an immutable Git change set before claiming delivery.\n- Treat artifact declarations, Git observations and delivery verification as\n  separate evidence; none proves the others by itself.\n- For typed delivery targets, require a path from reconciled artifacts through\n  a production entrypoint to current structured validation results. A surface\n  requires reachability plus integration/e2e evidence; a composed capability\n  requires integration evidence.\n- Evidence answers for the component it ran in or for a component inside it: a\n  module-wide run covers its subtree, but a result from a directory inside a\n  component does not answer for the whole component. Coverage that rests only\n  on a containing module is reported as `inferred-coverage` — name it as such.\n\n## Blocking anti-patterns\n\n- Claiming `100% COMPLETE` or production readiness without a green\n  `pose validate --strict` for every affected module.\n- Publishing contradictory delivery documents for the same scope; reconcile them first.\n- Merging code with completion documentation before the applicable POSE checks pass.\n- Mixing aspiration and verified state in one paragraph without clear labels.\n\n## Minimum checks\n\n- Run `pose check --strict` for structure and spec status enums.\n- Run `pose validate --strict` for every module the document claims to deliver.\n- Run `pose lint-spec` when the document is a spec.\n- Run `pose artifact-check --strict` when the scope changes governed source-tree paths.\n- Run `pose surface-check --strict` for specs with `delivers` and `pose\n  roadmap-check --strict` before a roadmap claims completion.\n- `pose assess discover --if-stale --update-state` when closing specs to update completeness metrics, technical debt, and reports under `.pose/assessments/` and `.pose/state/`.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence over progress narratives when rules conflict.\n- When pressured to claim completion without a gate, record the real state\n  (`in-progress` or `blocked`) and the remaining exit conditions.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/AGENTS.md\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/AGENTS.md\n ...pose-federated-proof-reads-each-input-once.json |    8 +\n .pose/indexes/delivery-integrity.json              | 6204 +++++++++++---------\n .pose/indexes/generation.json                      |   18 +-\n .pose/indexes/spec-graph.json                      | 1164 ++--\n .pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json               |    7 +\n .pose/results/delivery-validation.json             | 1653 +++---\n .../5a5c40e21c5b6920c47373d6/000001.json           |   11 +\n .../5a5c40e21c5b6920c47373d6/000001.result.json    |    6 +\n .../9696ec250024f6aac3f4f52a/000001.json           |   11 +\n .../9696ec250024f6aac3f4f52a/000001.result.json    |    6 +\n .../review-attestations/rva-87d5bfabc01ff84e.json  |  220 +\n .../review-attestations/rva-923a9017fb572ebd.json  |  232 +\n .pose/review-bundles/rvb-e8538ee1e24ad7ef.json     | 3321 +++++++++++\n .pose/review-bundles/rvb-f53880462209d2b9.json     | 3412 +++++++++++\n .pose/review-conclusions/rrn-4a532feec7405caf.json |   13 +\n .pose/review-conclusions/rrn-a37f896b2e3bf62f.json |   13 +\n .pose/review-run-ledger/rrn-4a532feec7405caf.json  |    8 +\n .pose/review-run-ledger/rrn-a37f896b2e3bf62f.json  |    8 +\n ...b81ad8b6307b0baf015953bc0986b6a2fcc4ddd23a.json |   23 +\n ...5b9ddf3e9da649991599285ad776626885bc14ecde.json |   23 +\n .../review-runs/rrn-4a532feec7405caf.events.ndjson |   69 +\n .pose/review-runs/rrn-4a532feec7405caf.json        |  136 +\n .pose/review-runs/rrn-4a532feec7405caf.transcript  |   70 +\n .../review-runs/rrn-a37f896b2e3bf62f.events.ndjson |   43 +\n .pose/review-runs/rrn-a37f896b2e3bf62f.json        |  142 +\n .pose/review-runs/rrn-a37f896b2e3bf62f.transcript  |   44 +\n ...5-pose-federated-proof-reads-each-input-once.md |  167 +\n ...pose-federated-proof-reads-each-input-once.json |   68 +\n pose-mcp/internal/cli/material_engine.go           |   18 +-\n .../internal/pose/current_material_cache_test.go   |   97 +\n pose-mcp/internal/pose/kept_cache.go               |   84 +\n pose-mcp/internal/pose/kept_cache_test.go          |  148 +\n pose-mcp/internal/pose/material_binding.go         |   52 +-\n pose-mcp/internal/pose/release_archive.go          |   16 +-\n .../internal/pose/release_manifest_integrity.go    |  110 +-\n .../internal/pose/release_snapshot_cache_test.go   |  182 +\n .../pose/review_bundle_header_cache_test.go        |   44 +\n pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_operation_view.go    |   53 +-\n 38 files changed, 13745 insertions(+), 4159 deletions(-)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"rg --files .pose/specs .pose/rules .pose/knowledge | rg 'federated-proof|backend-go|documentation-style|cache|material'; cat .pose/specs/*federated-proof-reads-each-input-once.md; git diff bf2ff02..8cb6cf3 -- pose-mcp/internal/pose/kept_cache.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/material_binding.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/release_manifest_integrity.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/release_archive.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_operation_view.go pose-mcp/internal/cli/material_engine.go\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"rg --files .pose/specs .pose/rules .pose/knowledge | rg 'federated-proof|backend-go|documentation-style|cache|material'; cat .pose/specs/*federated-proof-reads-each-input-once.md; git diff bf2ff02..8cb6cf3 -- pose-mcp/internal/pose/kept_cache.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/material_binding.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/release_manifest_integrity.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/release_archive.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_operation_view.go pose-mcp/internal/cli/material_engine.go\"","aggregated_output":".pose/rules/documentation-style.md\n.pose/rules/backend-go.md\n.pose/specs/2026-10-05-pose-validation-check-additions-are-not-material.md\n.pose/specs/2026-11-14-pose-index-material-does-not-ask-closeout.md\n.pose/specs/2026-10-14-pose-bounded-isolated-graph-caches.md\n.pose/specs/2026-11-07-pose-validation-evidence-material-binding.md\n.pose/specs/2026-10-04-pose-material-equivalence-reuse.md\n.pose/specs/2026-10-13-pose-residual-material-coverage-and-identity.md\n.pose/specs/2026-11-09-pose-worktree-runtime-resident-cache.md\n.pose/specs/2026-11-15-pose-federated-proof-reads-each-input-once.md\n---\nslug: pose-federated-proof-reads-each-input-once\nstatus: in-progress\ncreated_at: 2026-11-15\ncompleted_at:        # stamped on the transition to status: done\nsupersedes:          # slug of the superseded spec (when applicable)\ndepends_on:          # prerequisites, inline list: other-spec, milestone:<roadmap>/<id>, roadmap:<slug>\nremediates:          # optional explicit links: spec:slug@defect-fix, finding:rva-<16hex>/F1@revert (never inferred from depends_on)\npriority:            # integer >= 0 (lower = higher priority); ordering preference, not a blocker\ncomponents: pose-mcp\ntask_type: bugfix\nsurface:             # optional: minimal | standard | full (default) — minimal drops Tasks and derivable report sections; `pose specs facts <slug>` derives them\ndelivers: capability:federated-proof-reads-each-input-once\n---\n\n# Spec: pose-federated-proof-reads-each-input-once\n\n## 1. Intent\n\n### Goal\nFazer a prova federada de um projeto que depende do pose-dist ler cada entrada imutável uma vez por processo: o material de um change set num commit, o snapshot de uma release num commit, o commit a que o objeto de uma tag chega e o escopo que o conteúdo de um bundle declara.\n\n### Business value\nAo fixar o pose-dist em `bf2ff02` com o pin de federação certo, o `pose index` do harne8 passou a levar 23 minutos (1.383 s) e o `check --tolerant` do pre-commit chegou a 11 GB em um minuto, com 16 workers. Enquanto o pin estava velho (`485d59a`), a prova federada parava cedo na divergência de revisão e o custo ficava escondido. Medido pelo caminho que a ferramenta toma (contagem de processos git e perfil de CPU): a preparação de cada bundle de spec recalculava o material de todos os change sets do grafo com um `ls-tree` por set (20 mil processos em três minutos); cada leitura das releases arquivadas relia cada snapshot blob a blob e resolvia cada tag duas vezes, com três processos por tag; e cada consulta por escopo relia e reparseava o JSON de todos os bundles (63% da CPU).\n\n### Constraints\n- Nenhum resultado muda: os índices do harne8 gerados com e sem a mudança são idênticos byte a byte.\n- Só entra no cache o que é função de conteúdo endereçado: um commit, um objeto de tag, os bytes de um bundle. Uma ref (HEAD, tag) é lida a cada vez.\n\n### Non-goals\n- O recálculo do material federado por escopo dentro do `check` (follow-up F1).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- R1: The current material of a change set shall be computed once per process for the same inputs at the same commit, with HEAD resolved once per graph; a HEAD that cannot be resolved shall be read per set and not kept.\n- R2: The snapshot of a release tag at a commit shall be read from git once per process; each reader shall receive its own copy, and a failing git read shall not be kept.\n- R3: The commit a tag object peels to, and whether it is annotated, shall be kept per object, while the tag's ref is read every time; the archived releases shall list the tags' refs in one git process and read each snapshot at the commit already resolved, answering as `ResolveReleaseTag` answers each tag alone.\n- R4: What a review bundle's bytes declare about their scope shall be parsed once per content digest, each file keeping its own artifact path in a loss.\n- R5: The kept entries of every cache shall be bounded for a long-running process.\n- R6: A key missed by several callers together shall be computed once; a result that records a failing read (an unreadable material, a snapshot with gaps, a failed peel) shall not be kept, and a shallow checkout shall read its material uncached.\n\n### Non-functional\n- O `pose index` do harne8 com o pose-dist em `bf2ff02` cai de 1.383 s para no máximo 300 s, com índices idênticos.\n\n### Security\n- Nenhuma mudança: a chave de cada cache é o conteúdo ou o objeto, e uma ref nunca é cacheada.\n\n### Compatibility\n- Nenhum resultado muda.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Technical Plan\n\n### Affected areas\n- `pose/material_binding.go` e `cli/material_engine.go`: HEAD resolvido por grafo e digest guardado por (raiz, commit, entradas).\n- `pose/release_manifest_integrity.go` e `pose/release_archive.go`: snapshot por commit, resolução de tag por objeto e listagem única das refs.\n- `pose/review_operation_view.go`: parse do cabeçalho por digest do conteúdo.\n\n### Artifacts\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/material_binding.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/material_engine.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/release_manifest_integrity.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/release_archive.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_operation_view.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/current_material_cache_test.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/release_snapshot_cache_test.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_bundle_header_cache_test.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/kept_cache.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/kept_cache_test.go\n- modified: .pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\n\n### Delivery targets\n\n- capability:federated-proof-reads-each-input-once module:pose-mcp profile:composed-capability entrypoint:pose-mcp/cmd/pose/main.go\n\nComposto em produção por `pose index`, `pose check` e por toda verificação de bundle federado. Check permanente `federated-proof-reads-each-input-once-integration`, evidenceClass `integration`, required: `go test ./internal/pose -run 'TestCurrentMaterialIsReadOncePerCommit|TestCurrentMaterialFollowsAMovedHead|TestCurrentMaterialWithoutAHeadIsNotKept|TestCurrentMaterialKeptDigestsAreBounded|TestEvidenceIsJudgedAgainstTheCurrentMaterial|TestAReleaseSnapshotIsReadOncePerCommit|TestAKeptReleaseSnapshotIsCopiedToEachReader|TestAMovedReleaseTagIsReadAtItsNewCommit|TestAFailedReleaseSnapshotReadIsNotKept|TestAReleaseTagPeelIsKeptPerObject|TestAListedReleaseTagResolvesAsAloneOne|TestABundleHeaderIsParsedOncePerContent|TestAKeptValueIsComputedOnceForConcurrentMisses|TestADeclinedKeptValueIsComputedAgain|TestAnUnreadableMaterialIsAskedAgain|TestASnapshotWithGapsIsReadAgain|TestAFailedReleaseTagPeelIsAskedAgain|TestARecoveredReadIsComputedOnceAndKept|TestABoundCrossingKeepsAPendingKey' -count=1`.\n\n### API/contract changes\n- Nenhuma.\n\n### Data/storage changes\n- Nenhuma.\n\n### Technical risks\n- Um cache com chave errada daria um resultado velho; cada chave é um conteúdo endereçado, e os testes provam a tag movida, o HEAD que andou e o conteúdo mudado.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Tasks\n\n### Implementation\n- [x] Material por commit (R1), snapshot por commit (R2), tag por objeto e listagem única (R3), cabeçalho por conteúdo (R4), limites (R5).\n\n### Validation\n- [x] Testes de cada cache, com prova de reprovação; índices do harne8 idênticos antes e depois; tempo medido.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Decisions\n\n### Decision D1\n- Date: 2026-11-15\n- Context: o primeiro cache (snapshots) não mudou o tempo sozinho, nem o segundo (material) sozinho; cada um só deslocou o gargalo para o outro, e os dumps de goroutines, por amostrarem instantes, apontaram fases que não eram as dominantes.\n- Options considered: (a) parar no primeiro cache sem efeito; (b) medir cada passo pelo caminho da ferramenta (processos git e perfil de CPU) e manter só os caches que, juntos, mudam o tempo.\n- Decision: (b).\n- Rationale: medidos juntos, material e snapshots levaram o `index` de 1.383 s a 959 s; tags por objeto e listagem única, a 546 s; o parse por conteúdo, a 249 s.\n- Consequences: cada cache tem a chave no conteúdo endereçado, e nenhuma ref entra em cache.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Validation\n\n### Strategy\nTeste de cada cache com prova de reprovação; os índices do harne8 gerados por cada variante comparados byte a byte com os do motor sem mudança; tempo do `index` medido em cada passo.\n\n### Deterministic checks\n\n#### Test\n- Command: `go test ./internal/pose ./internal/cli ./internal/mcpserver ./internal/scaffold -count=1`\n- Scope: pose-mcp\n- Expected: verde\n\n### Execution log\n- Date: 2026-11-15\n- Environment: Linux, Go 1.26, 16 núcleos; harne8 com o pose-dist em bf2ff02 e as raízes que `scripts/pose-cli-local.sh` injeta.\n- Notes: `pose index` do harne8: 1.383 s sem mudança; 1.496 s só com o cache de snapshots; 1.530 s só com o de material; 959 s com os dois; 570 s com tags por objeto; 546 s com a listagem única; 249 s com o parse por conteúdo. Os três índices (`roadmaps.json`, `spec-graph.json`, `delivery-integrity.json`) idênticos em todas as variantes. Processos git em três minutos: 45 mil (20 mil `ls-tree HEAD`) sem mudança, 14 mil com os três primeiros caches. O `check --tolerant` com 4 workers termina (37 min, 2,9 GB); com 16 passa de 11 GB: o recálculo do material federado por escopo é o follow-up F1.\n\n### Results summary\n- Successes: `index` 5,5 vezes mais rápido com resultados idênticos.\n- Failures: nenhuma.\n- Warnings: o `check` continua inviável no pre-commit do harne8 até o F1.\n\n### Requirement trace\n- R1 [satisfied] test:TestCurrentMaterialIsReadOncePerCommit test:TestCurrentMaterialFollowsAMovedHead test:TestCurrentMaterialWithoutAHeadIsNotKept test:TestEvidenceIsJudgedAgainstTheCurrentMaterial\n- R2 [satisfied] test:TestAReleaseSnapshotIsReadOncePerCommit test:TestAKeptReleaseSnapshotIsCopiedToEachReader test:TestAMovedReleaseTagIsReadAtItsNewCommit test:TestAFailedReleaseSnapshotReadIsNotKept\n- R3 [satisfied] test:TestAReleaseTagPeelIsKeptPerObject test:TestAListedReleaseTagResolvesAsAloneOne\n- R4 [satisfied] test:TestABundleHeaderIsParsedOncePerContent\n- R6 [satisfied] test:TestAKeptValueIsComputedOnceForConcurrentMisses test:TestADeclinedKeptValueIsComputedAgain test:TestARecoveredReadIsComputedOnceAndKept test:TestABoundCrossingKeepsAPendingKey test:TestAnUnreadableMaterialIsAskedAgain test:TestASnapshotWithGapsIsReadAgain test:TestAFailedReleaseTagPeelIsAskedAgain\n- R5 [satisfied] test:TestCurrentMaterialKeptDigestsAreBounded check:federated-proof-reads-each-input-once-integration\n\n### Known gaps\n- O limite do cache de cabeçalhos (R5) não tem teste próprio; é o mesmo mecanismo do de material, que tem.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Final Report\n\n### Delivered scope\n\nNão concluída.\n\n### Residual risks\n\n### Follow-ups\n\n- [open] F1: o `check` recalcula o material federado inteiro para cada escopo (`checkReviewCloseout` → `ReadIndex` → `checkIndexInputs` → `BuildIndexFederatedMaterialIn`), e cada worker retém a própria read view: no harne8, 37 min com 4 workers e mais de 11 GB com 16. Calcular o material uma vez por processo, com chave no fechamento de entradas da read view, e limitar a memória por worker. (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:high review:2026-11-22)\n\n### Review\n\nRodada 1 (run rrn-4a532feec7405caf, attestation rva-923a9017fb572ebd, changes-requested): 4 bloqueadores, tratados. F1 o cache de material guardava o marcador `unreadable` e não conferia o clone raso: o estado de clone raso passou a ser conferido a cada resolução do HEAD (um repositório raso lê sem cache), e um material ilegível não é guardado. F2 o cache de snapshots guardava snapshots com gaps, que podem registrar uma leitura do git que falhou: só um snapshot íntegro, ou um commit sem manifesto, é guardado. F3 uma resolução de tag que falhou era guardada como \"não é commit\": só uma resolução que chegou a um commit é guardada. F4 duas chamadas que perdiam a mesma chave calculavam as duas: os quatro caches passaram a um `keptCache` que coordena o trabalho em andamento por chave (quem chega espera o primeiro), e um resultado que não é guardado é recalculado por quem esperava. Um teste por achado, cada um reprova com o comportamento anterior.\n\nRodada 2 (run rrn-a37f896b2e3bf62f, attestation rva-87d5bfabc01ff84e, changes-requested): F1 a F3 corrigidos, F4 parcial, e 2 bloqueadores, tratados. N1 quem esperava um resultado não guardado calculava por fora, sem publicar um sucesso depois da recuperação: volta a adquirir a chave pelo cache, e a leitura recuperada é calculada uma vez e guardada. N2 o despejo por capacidade trocava o mapa inteiro, inclusive chaves ainda em cálculo: só os valores concluídos saem, e uma chave em cálculo mantém a entrada. Um teste para cada, e os dois reprovam com o helper da rodada 1.\n\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/material_engine.go b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/material_engine.go\nindex 3d2af579..5eadca82 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/material_engine.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/material_engine.go\n@@ -1,14 +1,28 @@\n package cli\n \n import (\n+\t\"strings\"\n+\n \tposemodel \"github.com/harne8/pose-mcp/internal/pose\"\n \t\"github.com/harne8/pose-mcp/internal/version\"\n )\n \n func init() {\n \tposemodel.SetEngineVersion(version.Version)\n-\tposemodel.SetCurrentMaterialDigests(func(root string, in posemodel.MaterialInputs) string {\n+\tposemodel.SetCurrentMaterialAt(func(root string) string {\n+\t\t// A shallow checkout has no commit-bound material: it reads HEAD\n+\t\t// uncached, and is unreadable (review round 1, F1).\n+\t\tgit := func(limit int, args ...string) ([]byte, error) { return gitOutputBounded(root, limit, args...) }\n+\t\tif shallow, err := repositoryIsShallow(git, root); err != nil || shallow {\n+\t\t\treturn \"\"\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tout, err := gitOutputBounded(root, 1024, \"rev-parse\", \"--verify\", \"HEAD\")\n+\t\tif err != nil {\n+\t\t\treturn \"\"\n+\t\t}\n+\t\treturn strings.TrimSpace(string(out))\n+\t}, func(root, head string, in posemodel.MaterialInputs) string {\n \t\tgit := func(limit int, args ...string) ([]byte, error) { return gitOutputBounded(root, limit, args...) }\n-\t\treturn materialSetDigestAt(git, root, \"HEAD\", in)\n+\t\treturn materialSetDigestAt(git, root, head, in)\n \t})\n }\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/kept_cache.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/kept_cache.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000..ede86728\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/kept_cache.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,84 @@\n+package pose\n+\n+import \"sync\"\n+\n+// keptCache holds values computed from content-addressed inputs for the\n+// process (spec pose-federated-proof-reads-each-input-once). A key is\n+// computed once even when several callers miss it together: the first\n+// computes, the others wait for it. A result the computation declines to\n+// keep (a failed read) is not shared: the waiters acquire the key again.\n+// Past its bound the kept values are dropped, for a long-running process;\n+// a key being computed keeps its entry.\n+type keptCache[V any] struct {\n+\tmu      sync.Mutex\n+\tentries map[string]*keptEntry[V]\n+\tbound   int\n+}\n+\n+type keptEntry[V any] struct {\n+\tready chan struct{}\n+\tvalue V\n+\tkept  bool\n+\tdone  bool\n+}\n+\n+func newKeptCache[V any](bound int) *keptCache[V] {\n+\treturn &keptCache[V]{entries: map[string]*keptEntry[V]{}, bound: bound}\n+}\n+\n+// get returns the kept value of key, or computes it; compute reports whether\n+// its result may be kept. A caller that waited for a result that was not\n+// kept acquires the key again, so a recovered read is computed once and\n+// shared (review round 2, N1).\n+func (c *keptCache[V]) get(key string, compute func() (V, bool)) V {\n+\tfor {\n+\t\tc.mu.Lock()\n+\t\tif entry, ok := c.entries[key]; ok {\n+\t\t\tc.mu.Unlock()\n+\t\t\t<-entry.ready\n+\t\t\tif entry.kept {\n+\t\t\t\treturn entry.value\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tc.evictLocked()\n+\t\tentry := &keptEntry[V]{ready: make(chan struct{})}\n+\t\tc.entries[key] = entry\n+\t\tc.mu.Unlock()\n+\t\tvalue, keep := compute()\n+\t\tc.mu.Lock()\n+\t\tentry.value, entry.kept, entry.done = value, keep, true\n+\t\tif !keep && c.entries[key] == entry {\n+\t\t\tdelete(c.entries, key)\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tc.mu.Unlock()\n+\t\tclose(entry.ready)\n+\t\treturn value\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+// evictLocked drops the kept values once the bound is reached; a key still\n+// being computed keeps its entry, so its waiters and a later caller never\n+// compute it twice (review round 2, N2).\n+func (c *keptCache[V]) evictLocked() {\n+\tif len(c.entries) < c.bound {\n+\t\treturn\n+\t}\n+\tfor key, entry := range c.entries {\n+\t\tif entry.done {\n+\t\t\tdelete(c.entries, key)\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+func (c *keptCache[V]) clear() {\n+\tc.mu.Lock()\n+\tc.entries = map[string]*keptEntry[V]{}\n+\tc.mu.Unlock()\n+}\n+\n+func (c *keptCache[V]) len() int {\n+\tc.mu.Lock()\n+\tdefer c.mu.Unlock()\n+\treturn len(c.entries)\n+}\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/material_binding.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/material_binding.go\nindex 71cd4b4b..254a6083 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/material_binding.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/material_binding.go\n@@ -5,6 +5,7 @@ import (\n \t\"encoding/hex\"\n \t\"encoding/json\"\n \t\"path\"\n+\t\"path/filepath\"\n \t\"sort\"\n \t\"strings\"\n )\n@@ -231,16 +232,63 @@ func SetCurrentMaterialDigests(f func(root string, inputs MaterialInputs) string\n \tCurrentMaterialDigests = f\n }\n \n+// currentMaterialHead resolves a repository's HEAD to its commit, and\n+// currentMaterialAt digests a set's inputs in that commit's tree; the binary\n+// registers both. A commit names its tree by content, so the digest of the\n+// same inputs at the same commit is kept for the process: a review bundle\n+// recomputes the material of every change set of the graph for every spec it\n+// prepares, and a governance check prepares bundles for every scope (spec\n+// pose-federated-proof-reads-each-input-once).\n+var (\n+\tcurrentMaterialHead func(root string) string\n+\tcurrentMaterialAt   func(root, head string, inputs MaterialInputs) string\n+\t// currentMaterialKept bounds the kept digests of a long-running process\n+\t// (the MCP server sees HEAD move): past the bound, they are dropped.\n+\tcurrentMaterialKept = newKeptCache[string](20000)\n+)\n+\n+// SetCurrentMaterialAt registers the commit-bound recomputation.\n+func SetCurrentMaterialAt(head func(root string) string, at func(root, head string, inputs MaterialInputs) string) {\n+\tcurrentMaterialHead, currentMaterialAt = head, at\n+}\n+\n func withCurrentMaterial(root string, sets []ChangeSet) []ChangeSet {\n-\tif CurrentMaterialDigests == nil {\n+\tif CurrentMaterialDigests == nil && currentMaterialAt == nil {\n \t\treturn sets\n \t}\n+\t// HEAD is resolved once for the whole graph; a set's digest is then the\n+\t// digest at that commit. Unresolvable HEAD falls back to reading the tree\n+\t// of HEAD per set, uncached.\n+\thead := \"\"\n+\tif CurrentMaterialDigests == nil && currentMaterialHead != nil {\n+\t\thead = currentMaterialHead(root)\n+\t}\n \tout := make([]ChangeSet, len(sets))\n \tfor i, set := range sets {\n \t\tif set.MaterialInputs != nil && set.MaterialDigest != \"\" && set.MaterialDigest != MaterialDigestUnreadable {\n-\t\t\tset.MaterialDigest = CurrentMaterialDigests(root, *set.MaterialInputs)\n+\t\t\tset.MaterialDigest = currentMaterialDigest(root, head, *set.MaterialInputs)\n \t\t}\n \t\tout[i] = set\n \t}\n \treturn out\n }\n+\n+func currentMaterialDigest(root, head string, inputs MaterialInputs) string {\n+\tif CurrentMaterialDigests != nil {\n+\t\treturn CurrentMaterialDigests(root, inputs)\n+\t}\n+\tif !isGitRevision(head) {\n+\t\treturn currentMaterialAt(root, \"HEAD\", inputs)\n+\t}\n+\tencoded, err := json.Marshal(inputs)\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn currentMaterialAt(root, head, inputs)\n+\t}\n+\tkey := filepath.Clean(root) + \"\\x00\" + head + \"\\x00\" + string(encoded)\n+\t// An unreadable tree is not the commit's answer: it is asked again\n+\t// (review round 1, F1).\n+\treturn currentMaterialKept.get(key, func() (string, bool) {\n+\t\tdigest := currentMaterialAt(root, head, inputs)\n+\t\treturn digest, digest != \"\" && digest != MaterialDigestUnreadable\n+\t})\n+}\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/release_archive.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/release_archive.go\nindex aed583a0..fbcf8818 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/release_archive.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/release_archive.go\n@@ -59,6 +59,7 @@ func LoadArchivedFragmentsIn(files Files, root string) []ArchivedFragment {\n \t\treturn nil\n \t}\n \tarchived := []ArchivedFragment{}\n+\trefs, listed := releaseTagRefs(root)\n \tfor _, entry := range entries {\n \t\tif !entry.IsDir() || ValidateReleaseVersion(entry.Name()) != nil {\n \t\t\tcontinue\n@@ -76,8 +77,19 @@ func LoadArchivedFragmentsIn(files Files, root string) []ArchivedFragment {\n \t\t// A release that has a tag is the cut the tag holds: a manifest of the working tree that is\n \t\t// not that cut attests nothing.\n \t\tboundToTag := true\n-\t\tif _, _, tagErr := ResolveReleaseTag(root, entry.Name()); tagErr == nil {\n-\t\t\tif snap, snapErr := ReadReleaseSnapshot(root, entry.Name()); snapErr != nil || ReleaseDigest(string(manifestRaw)) != snap.ManifestDigest {\n+\t\t// The tags' refs are listed once for all releases, and the snapshot is\n+\t\t// read at the commit the tag was resolved to (spec\n+\t\t// pose-federated-proof-reads-each-input-once).\n+\t\tvar commit string\n+\t\tvar annotated bool\n+\t\tvar tagErr error\n+\t\tif listed {\n+\t\t\tcommit, annotated, tagErr = resolveListedReleaseTag(root, entry.Name(), refs)\n+\t\t} else {\n+\t\t\tcommit, annotated, tagErr = ResolveReleaseTag(root, entry.Name())\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif tagErr == nil {\n+\t\t\tif snap, snapErr := readReleaseSnapshotAtCommit(root, entry.Name(), commit, annotated); snapErr != nil || ReleaseDigest(string(manifestRaw)) != snap.ManifestDigest {\n \t\t\t\tboundToTag = false\n \t\t\t}\n \t\t} else if !errors.Is(tagErr, ErrReleaseTagMissing) {\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/release_manifest_integrity.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/release_manifest_integrity.go\nindex 39b5938e..41ca1880 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/release_manifest_integrity.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/release_manifest_integrity.go\n@@ -351,7 +351,8 @@ func ResolveReleaseTag(root, tag string) (commit string, annotated bool, err err\n \tif ValidateReleaseVersion(tag) != nil {\n \t\treturn \"\", false, fmt.Errorf(\"pose: %q is not a release tag\", tag)\n \t}\n-\tif _, verr := gitOutput(root, \"rev-parse\", \"--verify\", \"--quiet\", \"refs/tags/\"+tag); verr != nil {\n+\tobject, verr := gitOutput(root, \"rev-parse\", \"--verify\", \"--quiet\", \"refs/tags/\"+tag)\n+\tif verr != nil {\n \t\tif gitNotARepository(root) {\n \t\t\t// No repository: there is no tag, which is a different thing from a tag Git could not read.\n \t\t\treturn \"\", false, fmt.Errorf(\"%w: %s (not a git repository)\", ErrReleaseTagMissing, tag)\n@@ -366,15 +367,73 @@ func ResolveReleaseTag(root, tag string) (commit string, annotated bool, err err\n \t\t}\n \t\treturn \"\", false, fmt.Errorf(\"pose: the tag %s could not be resolved: %w\", tag, verr)\n \t}\n-\tkind, err := gitOutput(root, \"cat-file\", \"-t\", \"refs/tags/\"+tag)\n+\t// The ref is read every time, since a tag can move; what the object it\n+\t// names is, and the commit it peels to, never change, and are kept per\n+\t// object (spec pose-federated-proof-reads-each-input-once).\n+\treturn peelReleaseTagObject(root, tag, strings.TrimSpace(object))\n+}\n+\n+// peelReleaseTagObject resolves the object a release tag's ref names to the\n+// commit it peels to, and whether it is an annotated tag.\n+func peelReleaseTagObject(root, tag, object string) (commit string, annotated bool, err error) {\n+\t// Only a peel that reached a commit is kept: a failed inspection or peel\n+\t// may be an object not fetched yet, or a failing git, and is asked again\n+\t// (review round 1, F3).\n+\tpeeled := releaseTagObjects.get(filepath.Clean(root)+\"\\x00\"+object, func() (releaseTagObject, bool) {\n+\t\tkind, err := gitOutput(root, \"cat-file\", \"-t\", object)\n+\t\tif err != nil {\n+\t\t\treturn releaseTagObject{err: fmt.Errorf(\"pose: the tag %s could not be inspected: %w\", tag, err)}, false\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tout, err := gitOutput(root, \"rev-parse\", \"--verify\", object+\"^{commit}\")\n+\t\tif err != nil {\n+\t\t\treturn releaseTagObject{err: fmt.Errorf(\"%w: %s\", ErrReleaseTagNotCommit, tag)}, false\n+\t\t}\n+\t\treturn releaseTagObject{commit: strings.TrimSpace(out), annotated: strings.TrimSpace(kind) == \"tag\"}, isGitRevision(object)\n+\t})\n+\treturn peeled.commit, peeled.annotated, peeled.err\n+}\n+\n+// releaseTagRefs lists, in one git process, the object every tag's ref\n+// names. ok is false when git cannot answer; the caller then reads each tag\n+// on its own.\n+func releaseTagRefs(root string) (map[string]string, bool) {\n+\tout, err := gitOutput(root, \"for-each-ref\", \"--format=%(refname)%00%(objectname)\", \"refs/tags/\")\n \tif err != nil {\n-\t\treturn \"\", false, fmt.Errorf(\"pose: the tag %s could not be inspected: %w\", tag, err)\n+\t\treturn nil, false\n \t}\n-\tout, err := gitOutput(root, \"rev-parse\", \"--verify\", \"refs/tags/\"+tag+\"^{commit}\")\n-\tif err != nil {\n-\t\treturn \"\", false, fmt.Errorf(\"%w: %s\", ErrReleaseTagNotCommit, tag)\n+\trefs := map[string]string{}\n+\tfor _, line := range strings.Split(out, \"\\n\") {\n+\t\tname, object, found := strings.Cut(line, \"\\x00\")\n+\t\tif !found {\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif tag, isTag := strings.CutPrefix(name, \"refs/tags/\"); isTag && isGitRevision(object) {\n+\t\t\trefs[tag] = object\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\treturn refs, true\n+}\n+\n+// resolveListedReleaseTag resolves a tag from a listing of the tags' refs,\n+// as ResolveReleaseTag reads it alone: a tag absent from the listing is\n+// missing.\n+func resolveListedReleaseTag(root, tag string, refs map[string]string) (string, bool, error) {\n+\tobject, ok := refs[tag]\n+\tif !ok {\n+\t\treturn \"\", false, fmt.Errorf(\"%w: %s\", ErrReleaseTagMissing, tag)\n \t}\n-\treturn strings.TrimSpace(out), strings.TrimSpace(kind) == \"tag\", nil\n+\treturn peelReleaseTagObject(root, tag, object)\n+}\n+\n+// releaseTagObjects keeps, per repository and tag object, the commit the\n+// object peels to (empty when it is not a commit) and whether it is an\n+// annotated tag: both are fixed by the object's content.\n+var releaseTagObjects = newKeptCache[releaseTagObject](10000)\n+\n+type releaseTagObject struct {\n+\tcommit    string\n+\tannotated bool\n+\terr       error\n }\n \n func gitOutput(root string, args ...string) (string, error) {\n@@ -556,7 +615,44 @@ func ReadReleaseSnapshot(root, tag string) (ReleaseSnapshot, error) {\n \treturn readReleaseSnapshotAtCommit(root, tag, commit, annotated)\n }\n \n+// releaseSnapshots holds, per process, the snapshot read for a tag at a\n+// commit: a commit names its tree by content, so the snapshot read from it\n+// never changes. Every review bundle a governance check prepares reads every\n+// archived release through here, one git process per blob (spec\n+// pose-federated-proof-reads-each-input-once). Only a snapshot read whole,\n+// or one the commit lacks, is kept: a failing git read is asked again. Each\n+// reader receives its own copy.\n+var releaseSnapshots = newKeptCache[releaseSnapshotEntry](10000)\n+\n+type releaseSnapshotEntry struct {\n+\tencoded []byte\n+\terr     error\n+}\n+\n func readReleaseSnapshotAtCommit(root, tag, commit string, annotated bool) (ReleaseSnapshot, error) {\n+\tkey := fmt.Sprintf(\"%s\\x00%s\\x00%s\\x00%t\", filepath.Clean(root), tag, commit, annotated)\n+\tvar fresh *ReleaseSnapshot\n+\tentry := releaseSnapshots.get(key, func() (releaseSnapshotEntry, bool) {\n+\t\tsnap, err := readReleaseSnapshotAtCommitUncached(root, tag, commit, annotated)\n+\t\tfresh = &snap\n+\t\tencoded, merr := json.Marshal(snap)\n+\t\t// Kept only when the read was whole: an intact snapshot, or a commit\n+\t\t// that holds no manifest. A snapshot with gaps may record a failing\n+\t\t// git read, and is read again (review round 1, F2).\n+\t\tkeep := merr == nil && commit != \"\" && (err == nil && snap.Intact() || errors.Is(err, ErrReleaseSnapshotMissing))\n+\t\treturn releaseSnapshotEntry{encoded: encoded, err: err}, keep\n+\t})\n+\tif fresh != nil {\n+\t\treturn *fresh, entry.err\n+\t}\n+\tvar snap ReleaseSnapshot\n+\tif err := json.Unmarshal(entry.encoded, &snap); err != nil {\n+\t\treturn readReleaseSnapshotAtCommitUncached(root, tag, commit, annotated)\n+\t}\n+\treturn snap, entry.err\n+}\n+\n+func readReleaseSnapshotAtCommitUncached(root, tag, commit string, annotated bool) (ReleaseSnapshot, error) {\n \tif commit == \"\" {\n \t\treturn ReleaseSnapshot{Tag: tag}, fmt.Errorf(\"pose: release tag %s has no captured commit\", tag)\n \t}\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_operation_view.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_operation_view.go\nindex 4515ab9f..9b8f34e9 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_operation_view.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_operation_view.go\n@@ -31,6 +31,25 @@ func readReviewBundleHeader(files Files, dir, name string) reviewHeaderObservati\n \t\treturn reviewHeaderObservation{loss: &loss, readError: err.Error()}\n \t}\n \tresult := reviewHeaderObservation{digest: sha256.Sum256(raw), inputBytes: len(raw)}\n+\t// What a bundle's bytes say about their scope is a function of the\n+\t// bytes: it is parsed once per content digest, so a governance check\n+\t// that reads every bundle for every scope parses each content once\n+\t// (spec pose-federated-proof-reads-each-input-once).\n+\tparsed := reviewBundleHeaderParses.get(string(result.digest[:]), func() (reviewBundleHeaderParse, bool) {\n+\t\treturn parseReviewBundleHeader(raw, rel), true\n+\t})\n+\tresult.owner = parsed.owner\n+\tif parsed.loss != nil {\n+\t\tloss := *parsed.loss\n+\t\tloss.Artifact = rel\n+\t\tresult.loss = &loss\n+\t}\n+\treturn result\n+}\n+\n+// parseReviewBundleHeader reads the scope a bundle's bytes declare, or why\n+// they declare none.\n+func parseReviewBundleHeader(raw []byte, rel string) reviewBundleHeaderParse {\n \tvar header struct {\n \t\tPayload struct {\n \t\t\tScope struct {\n@@ -38,19 +57,27 @@ func readReviewBundleHeader(files Files, dir, name string) reviewHeaderObservati\n \t\t\t} `json:\"scope\"`\n \t\t} `json:\"payload\"`\n \t}\n-\tif err := json.Unmarshal(raw, &header); err != nil {\n-\t\tloss := attributeLoss(SourceLoss{Producer: \"review-bundles\", Artifact: rel, Kind: LossParseError, Detail: \"the record is not valid JSON\"}, raw, \"payload\", \"scope\", \"ref\")\n-\t\tresult.loss = &loss\n-\t} else if _, _, ambiguous := sniffJSONString(raw, \"payload\", \"scope\", \"ref\"); ambiguous {\n-\t\tloss := SourceLoss{Producer: \"review-bundles\", Artifact: rel, Kind: LossInvalid, Detail: \"the record names its scope more than once\"}\n-\t\tresult.loss = &loss\n-\t} else if header.Payload.Scope.Ref == \"\" {\n-\t\tloss := SourceLoss{Producer: \"review-bundles\", Artifact: rel, Kind: LossParseError, Detail: \"the record names no scope\"}\n-\t\tresult.loss = &loss\n-\t} else {\n-\t\tresult.owner = header.Payload.Scope.Ref\n-\t}\n-\treturn result\n+\tvar loss SourceLoss\n+\tswitch {\n+\tcase json.Unmarshal(raw, &header) != nil:\n+\t\tloss = attributeLoss(SourceLoss{Producer: \"review-bundles\", Artifact: rel, Kind: LossParseError, Detail: \"the record is not valid JSON\"}, raw, \"payload\", \"scope\", \"ref\")\n+\tcase func() bool { _, _, ambiguous := sniffJSONString(raw, \"payload\", \"scope\", \"ref\"); return ambiguous }():\n+\t\tloss = SourceLoss{Producer: \"review-bundles\", Artifact: rel, Kind: LossInvalid, Detail: \"the record names its scope more than once\"}\n+\tcase header.Payload.Scope.Ref == \"\":\n+\t\tloss = SourceLoss{Producer: \"review-bundles\", Artifact: rel, Kind: LossParseError, Detail: \"the record names no scope\"}\n+\tdefault:\n+\t\treturn reviewBundleHeaderParse{owner: header.Payload.Scope.Ref}\n+\t}\n+\treturn reviewBundleHeaderParse{loss: &loss}\n+}\n+\n+// reviewBundleHeaderParses keeps, per content digest, the scope a bundle\n+// names or why it names none; bounded for a long-running process.\n+var reviewBundleHeaderParses = newKeptCache[reviewBundleHeaderParse](50000)\n+\n+type reviewBundleHeaderParse struct {\n+\towner string\n+\tloss  *SourceLoss\n }\n \n type reviewOperationView struct {\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat pose-mcp/internal/pose/kept_cache_test.go; cat pose-mcp/internal/pose/current_material_cache_test.go; cat pose-mcp/internal/pose/release_snapshot_cache_test.go; cat pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_bundle_header_cache_test.go; cat .pose/rules/backend-go.md; cat .pose/rules/documentation-style.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat pose-mcp/internal/pose/kept_cache_test.go; cat pose-mcp/internal/pose/current_material_cache_test.go; cat pose-mcp/internal/pose/release_snapshot_cache_test.go; cat pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_bundle_header_cache_test.go; cat .pose/rules/backend-go.md; cat .pose/rules/documentation-style.md'","aggregated_output":"package pose\n\nimport (\n\t\"sync\"\n\t\"sync/atomic\"\n\t\"testing\"\n)\n\n// Review round 1 (F4): callers that miss the same key together compute it\n// once; the others wait for the first.\nfunc TestAKeptValueIsComputedOnceForConcurrentMisses(t *testing.T) {\n\tcache := newKeptCache[int](10)\n\tvar computed atomic.Int32\n\trelease := make(chan struct{})\n\tvar started sync.WaitGroup\n\tstarted.Add(1)\n\tvar wg sync.WaitGroup\n\tresults := make([]int, 8)\n\tfor i := range results {\n\t\twg.Add(1)\n\t\tgo func() {\n\t\t\tdefer wg.Done()\n\t\t\tresults[i] = cache.get(\"k\", func() (int, bool) {\n\t\t\t\tif computed.Add(1) == 1 {\n\t\t\t\t\tstarted.Done()\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\t<-release\n\t\t\t\treturn 42, true\n\t\t\t})\n\t\t}()\n\t}\n\tstarted.Wait()\n\tclose(release)\n\twg.Wait()\n\tif computed.Load() != 1 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"eight concurrent misses computed %d times\", computed.Load())\n\t}\n\tfor i, v := range results {\n\t\tif v != 42 {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"caller %d got %d\", i, v)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n\n// F4: a result the computation declines to keep is not shared; the next\n// caller computes again, and a later success is kept.\nfunc TestADeclinedKeptValueIsComputedAgain(t *testing.T) {\n\tcache := newKeptCache[int](10)\n\tcalls := 0\n\tcompute := func() (int, bool) {\n\t\tcalls++\n\t\treturn calls, calls >= 2\n\t}\n\tif v := cache.get(\"k\", compute); v != 1 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"first %d\", v)\n\t}\n\tif v := cache.get(\"k\", compute); v != 2 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a declined result was reused: %d\", v)\n\t}\n\tif v := cache.get(\"k\", compute); v != 2 || calls != 2 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a kept result was computed again: %d after %d calls\", v, calls)\n\t}\n}\n\n// Review round 1 (F1): an unreadable material is asked again, and a\n// shallow checkout, which resolves no HEAD, is never kept.\nfunc TestAnUnreadableMaterialIsAskedAgain(t *testing.T) {\n\treads := 0\n\tanswer := MaterialDigestUnreadable\n\twithMaterialHooks(t, func(string) string { return \"0123456789012345678901234567890123456789\" }, func(string, string, MaterialInputs) string {\n\t\treads++\n\t\treturn answer\n\t})\n\tsets := materialSets(1)\n\tif got := withCurrentMaterial(\"/r\", sets)[0].MaterialDigest; got != MaterialDigestUnreadable {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"first %q\", got)\n\t}\n\tanswer = \"sha256:recovered\"\n\tif got := withCurrentMaterial(\"/r\", sets)[0].MaterialDigest; got != \"sha256:recovered\" || reads != 2 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the unreadable answer was kept: %q after %d reads\", got, reads)\n\t}\n}\n\n// Review round 2 (N1): callers that waited for a failed read retry through\n// the cache: the recovered read is computed once and kept.\nfunc TestARecoveredReadIsComputedOnceAndKept(t *testing.T) {\n\tcache := newKeptCache[int](10)\n\tvar calls atomic.Int32\n\trelease := make(chan struct{})\n\tfirst := make(chan struct{})\n\tcompute := func() (int, bool) {\n\t\tn := calls.Add(1)\n\t\tif n == 1 {\n\t\t\tclose(first)\n\t\t\t<-release\n\t\t\treturn 0, false\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn 7, true\n\t}\n\tvar wg sync.WaitGroup\n\tresults := make([]int, 6)\n\twg.Add(1)\n\tgo func() { defer wg.Done(); results[0] = cache.get(\"k\", compute) }()\n\t<-first\n\tfor i := 1; i < len(results); i++ {\n\t\twg.Add(1)\n\t\tgo func() { defer wg.Done(); results[i] = cache.get(\"k\", compute) }()\n\t}\n\tclose(release)\n\twg.Wait()\n\tif calls.Load() != 2 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a failure and its recovery computed %d times, want 2\", calls.Load())\n\t}\n\tif v := cache.get(\"k\", compute); v != 7 || calls.Load() != 2 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the recovered read was not kept: %d after %d calls\", v, calls.Load())\n\t}\n}\n\n// N2: crossing the bound while a key is computed keeps that key's entry, so\n// a later caller of the same key waits for it instead of computing again.\nfunc TestABoundCrossingKeepsAPendingKey(t *testing.T) {\n\tcache := newKeptCache[int](1)\n\tvar calls atomic.Int32\n\trelease := make(chan struct{})\n\tstarted := make(chan struct{})\n\tdone := make(chan int)\n\tgo func() {\n\t\tdone <- cache.get(\"a\", func() (int, bool) {\n\t\t\tcalls.Add(1)\n\t\t\tclose(started)\n\t\t\t<-release\n\t\t\treturn 1, true\n\t\t})\n\t}()\n\t<-started\n\tcache.get(\"b\", func() (int, bool) { return 2, true })\n\tsecond := make(chan int)\n\tgo func() {\n\t\tsecond <- cache.get(\"a\", func() (int, bool) { calls.Add(1); return 3, true })\n\t}()\n\tclose(release)\n\tif v := <-done; v != 1 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"first a %d\", v)\n\t}\n\tif v := <-second; v != 1 || calls.Load() != 1 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the pending key was computed again across the bound: %d after %d calls\", v, calls.Load())\n\t}\n}\npackage pose\n\nimport (\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"testing\"\n)\n\nfunc withMaterialHooks(t *testing.T, head func(string) string, at func(string, string, MaterialInputs) string) {\n\tt.Helper()\n\toldDigests, oldHead, oldAt := CurrentMaterialDigests, currentMaterialHead, currentMaterialAt\n\tCurrentMaterialDigests = nil\n\tSetCurrentMaterialAt(head, at)\n\tcurrentMaterialKept.clear()\n\tt.Cleanup(func() {\n\t\tCurrentMaterialDigests, currentMaterialHead, currentMaterialAt = oldDigests, oldHead, oldAt\n\t\tcurrentMaterialKept.clear()\n\t})\n}\n\nfunc materialSets(n int) []ChangeSet {\n\tsets := make([]ChangeSet, n)\n\tfor i := range sets {\n\t\tin := MaterialInputs{Names: []string{strings.Repeat(\"a\", i+1) + \".go\"}}\n\t\tsets[i] = ChangeSet{Spec: \"s\", MaterialDigest: \"sha256:indexed\", MaterialInputs: &in}\n\t}\n\treturn sets\n}\n\n// Spec pose-federated-proof-reads-each-input-once, R1: HEAD is resolved\n// once per graph, and the material of the same inputs at the same commit is\n// read from the tree once per process.\nfunc TestCurrentMaterialIsReadOncePerCommit(t *testing.T) {\n\theads, reads := 0, 0\n\twithMaterialHooks(t, func(string) string { heads++; return strings.Repeat(\"a\", 40) }, func(_, head string, in MaterialInputs) string {\n\t\treads++\n\t\treturn \"sha256:\" + head[:4] + in.Names[0]\n\t})\n\tsets := materialSets(5)\n\tfirst := withCurrentMaterial(\"/r\", sets)\n\tsecond := withCurrentMaterial(\"/r\", sets)\n\tif heads != 2 || reads != 5 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"heads=%d reads=%d, want 2 and 5\", heads, reads)\n\t}\n\tfor i := range first {\n\t\tif first[i].MaterialDigest != second[i].MaterialDigest || first[i].MaterialDigest == \"sha256:indexed\" {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"set %d: %q then %q\", i, first[i].MaterialDigest, second[i].MaterialDigest)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n\n// R2: a HEAD that moved is read at its new commit.\nfunc TestCurrentMaterialFollowsAMovedHead(t *testing.T) {\n\thead := strings.Repeat(\"a\", 40)\n\twithMaterialHooks(t, func(string) string { return head }, func(_, at string, in MaterialInputs) string { return \"sha256:\" + at[:1] })\n\tsets := materialSets(1)\n\tbefore := withCurrentMaterial(\"/r\", sets)[0].MaterialDigest\n\thead = strings.Repeat(\"b\", 40)\n\tafter := withCurrentMaterial(\"/r\", sets)[0].MaterialDigest\n\tif before != \"sha256:a\" || after != \"sha256:b\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"before %q after %q\", before, after)\n\t}\n}\n\n// R3: an unresolvable HEAD reads the tree of HEAD for each set and keeps\n// nothing.\nfunc TestCurrentMaterialWithoutAHeadIsNotKept(t *testing.T) {\n\treads := 0\n\twithMaterialHooks(t, func(string) string { return \"\" }, func(_, at string, in MaterialInputs) string {\n\t\treads++\n\t\tif at != \"HEAD\" {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"read at %q, want HEAD\", at)\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn \"sha256:head\"\n\t})\n\tsets := materialSets(2)\n\twithCurrentMaterial(\"/r\", sets)\n\twithCurrentMaterial(\"/r\", sets)\n\tif reads != 4 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"reads=%d, want 4\", reads)\n\t}\n}\n\n// R4: the kept digests of a long-running process are bounded.\nfunc TestCurrentMaterialKeptDigestsAreBounded(t *testing.T) {\n\thead := 0\n\twithMaterialHooks(t, func(string) string { head++; return strings.Repeat(string(rune('a'+head%6)), 40) }, func(string, string, MaterialInputs) string { return \"sha256:x\" })\n\told := currentMaterialKept.bound\n\tcurrentMaterialKept.bound = 3\n\tt.Cleanup(func() { currentMaterialKept.bound = old })\n\tfor i := 0; i < 10; i++ {\n\t\twithCurrentMaterial(\"/r\", materialSets(1))\n\t}\n\tkept := currentMaterialKept.len()\n\tif kept > 3 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"%d digests kept past a bound of 3\", kept)\n\t}\n}\npackage pose\n\nimport (\n\t\"errors\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"testing\"\n)\n\n// Spec pose-federated-proof-reads-each-input-once, R5: the snapshot of a\n// tag at a commit is read from git once per process; a second read of that\n// commit needs no git.\nfunc TestAReleaseSnapshotIsReadOncePerCommit(t *testing.T) {\n\tr := newReleaseRepo(t, \"v1.0.0\")\n\trelGit(t, r.root, \"tag\", \"v1.0.0\")\n\tcommit, annotated, err := ResolveReleaseTag(r.root, \"v1.0.0\")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tfirst, err := readReleaseSnapshotAtCommit(r.root, \"v1.0.0\", commit, annotated)\n\tif err != nil || !first.Intact() {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"first read: %+v %v\", first, err)\n\t}\n\t// Without the repository's objects, only the kept snapshot can answer.\n\tgit := filepath.Join(r.root, \".git\")\n\tif err := os.Rename(git, git+\".away\"); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tdefer os.Rename(git+\".away\", git)\n\tsecond, err := readReleaseSnapshotAtCommit(r.root, \"v1.0.0\", commit, annotated)\n\tif err != nil || !second.Intact() || second.ManifestDigest != first.ManifestDigest {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the second read of the same commit went back to git: %+v %v\", second, err)\n\t}\n}\n\n// R5: each reader receives its own copy; changing one does not change what\n// the next reader receives.\nfunc TestAKeptReleaseSnapshotIsCopiedToEachReader(t *testing.T) {\n\tr := newReleaseRepo(t, \"v1.0.0\")\n\trelGit(t, r.root, \"tag\", \"v1.0.0\")\n\tfirst, err := ReadReleaseSnapshot(r.root, \"v1.0.0\")\n\tif err != nil || first.Manifest == nil || len(first.Manifest.Fragments) == 0 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"first read: %+v %v\", first, err)\n\t}\n\tfirst.Gaps = append(first.Gaps, \"changed by a reader\")\n\tfirst.Manifest.Fragments[0].Spec = \"changed\"\n\tsecond, err := ReadReleaseSnapshot(r.root, \"v1.0.0\")\n\tif err != nil || len(second.Gaps) != 0 || second.Manifest.Fragments[0].Spec != \"alpha\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a reader's change reached the next reader: %+v %v\", second, err)\n\t}\n}\n\n// R5: a tag moved to another commit is read at that commit.\nfunc TestAMovedReleaseTagIsReadAtItsNewCommit(t *testing.T) {\n\tr := newReleaseRepo(t, \"v1.0.0\")\n\trelGit(t, r.root, \"tag\", \"v1.0.0\")\n\tbefore, err := ReadReleaseSnapshot(r.root, \"v1.0.0\")\n\tif err != nil || !before.Intact() {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"before: %+v %v\", before, err)\n\t}\n\trelWrite(t, r.root, \".pose/releases/v1.0.0/manifest.json\", \"{}\\n\")\n\tr.commit(t, \"break the manifest\")\n\trelGit(t, r.root, \"tag\", \"-f\", \"v1.0.0\")\n\tafter, err := ReadReleaseSnapshot(r.root, \"v1.0.0\")\n\tif after.Commit == before.Commit || after.Intact() {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the moved tag was read from the old commit's snapshot: %+v %v\", after, err)\n\t}\n}\n\n// R5: a git read that fails is asked again, not kept as the commit's answer.\nfunc TestAFailedReleaseSnapshotReadIsNotKept(t *testing.T) {\n\tr := newReleaseRepo(t, \"v1.0.0\")\n\trelGit(t, r.root, \"tag\", \"v1.0.0\")\n\tcommit, annotated, err := ResolveReleaseTag(r.root, \"v1.0.0\")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tgit := filepath.Join(r.root, \".git\")\n\tif err := os.Rename(git, git+\".away\"); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tif _, err := readReleaseSnapshotAtCommit(r.root, \"v1.0.0\", commit, annotated); err == nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(\"a read without the repository succeeded\")\n\t}\n\tif err := os.Rename(git+\".away\", git); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tif snap, err := readReleaseSnapshotAtCommit(r.root, \"v1.0.0\", commit, annotated); err != nil || !snap.Intact() {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the failed read was kept: %+v %v\", snap, err)\n\t}\n}\n\n// R6: a tag naming something that is not a commit is refused the same way\n// on every read, and a tag recreated as annotated reads as annotated.\nfunc TestAReleaseTagPeelIsKeptPerObject(t *testing.T) {\n\tr := newReleaseRepo(t, \"v1.0.0\")\n\ttree := relGit(t, r.root, \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD^{tree}\")\n\trelGit(t, r.root, \"tag\", \"v1.0.0\", tree)\n\tfor i := 0; i < 2; i++ {\n\t\tif _, _, err := ResolveReleaseTag(r.root, \"v1.0.0\"); !errors.Is(err, ErrReleaseTagNotCommit) {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"read %d: a tag of a tree resolved: %v\", i, err)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\trelGit(t, r.root, \"tag\", \"-d\", \"v1.0.0\")\n\trelGit(t, r.root, \"tag\", \"v1.0.0\")\n\tif _, annotated, err := ResolveReleaseTag(r.root, \"v1.0.0\"); err != nil || annotated {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"lightweight: %v %v\", annotated, err)\n\t}\n\trelGit(t, r.root, \"tag\", \"-d\", \"v1.0.0\")\n\trelGit(t, r.root, \"tag\", \"-a\", \"v1.0.0\", \"-m\", \"release\")\n\tif _, annotated, err := ResolveReleaseTag(r.root, \"v1.0.0\"); err != nil || !annotated {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"annotated: %v %v\", annotated, err)\n\t}\n}\n\n// R7: a tag resolved from the one listing of the tags' refs answers as\n// ResolveReleaseTag answers it alone: lightweight, annotated, missing, and\n// not a commit.\nfunc TestAListedReleaseTagResolvesAsAloneOne(t *testing.T) {\n\tr := newReleaseRepo(t, \"v1.0.0\")\n\tr.addVersion(t, \"v1.1.0\")\n\tr.addVersion(t, \"v1.2.0\")\n\trelGit(t, r.root, \"tag\", \"v1.0.0\")\n\trelGit(t, r.root, \"tag\", \"-a\", \"v1.1.0\", \"-m\", \"release\")\n\trelGit(t, r.root, \"tag\", \"v1.2.0\", relGit(t, r.root, \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD^{tree}\"))\n\trefs, ok := releaseTagRefs(r.root)\n\tif !ok {\n\t\tt.Fatal(\"the tags could not be listed\")\n\t}\n\tfor _, tag := range []string{\"v1.0.0\", \"v1.1.0\", \"v1.2.0\", \"v9.9.9\"} {\n\t\taloneCommit, aloneAnnotated, aloneErr := ResolveReleaseTag(r.root, tag)\n\t\tcommit, annotated, err := resolveListedReleaseTag(r.root, tag, refs)\n\t\tif commit != aloneCommit || annotated != aloneAnnotated || errors.Is(err, ErrReleaseTagMissing) != errors.Is(aloneErr, ErrReleaseTagMissing) || errors.Is(err, ErrReleaseTagNotCommit) != errors.Is(aloneErr, ErrReleaseTagNotCommit) || (err == nil) != (aloneErr == nil) {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"%s: listed %q %v %v, alone %q %v %v\", tag, commit, annotated, err, aloneCommit, aloneAnnotated, aloneErr)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n\n// Review round 1 (F3): a peel that failed is asked again once the object it\n// needs is available.\nfunc TestAFailedReleaseTagPeelIsAskedAgain(t *testing.T) {\n\tr := newReleaseRepo(t, \"v1.0.0\")\n\trelGit(t, r.root, \"tag\", \"v1.0.0\")\n\tobject := relGit(t, r.root, \"rev-parse\", \"refs/tags/v1.0.0\")\n\tgit := filepath.Join(r.root, \".git\")\n\tif err := os.Rename(git, git+\".away\"); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tif _, _, err := peelReleaseTagObject(r.root, \"v1.0.0\", object); err == nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(\"a peel without the repository succeeded\")\n\t}\n\tif err := os.Rename(git+\".away\", git); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tif commit, _, err := peelReleaseTagObject(r.root, \"v1.0.0\", object); err != nil || commit != object {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the failed peel was kept: %q %v\", commit, err)\n\t}\n}\n\n// Review round 1 (F2): a snapshot with gaps is read again, not kept.\nfunc TestASnapshotWithGapsIsReadAgain(t *testing.T) {\n\tr := newReleaseRepo(t, \"v1.0.0\")\n\trelWrite(t, r.root, \".pose/policy/release.json\", \"{}\\n\")\n\tr.commit(t, \"a release policy that does not satisfy its contract\")\n\trelGit(t, r.root, \"tag\", \"v1.0.0\")\n\tcommit, annotated, err := ResolveReleaseTag(r.root, \"v1.0.0\")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tfirst, _ := readReleaseSnapshotAtCommit(r.root, \"v1.0.0\", commit, annotated)\n\tif len(first.Gaps) == 0 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the fixture has no gap: %+v\", first)\n\t}\n\tgit := filepath.Join(r.root, \".git\")\n\tif err := os.Rename(git, git+\".away\"); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tdefer os.Rename(git+\".away\", git)\n\tif _, err := readReleaseSnapshotAtCommit(r.root, \"v1.0.0\", commit, annotated); err == nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(\"a snapshot with gaps was answered from the cache\")\n\t}\n}\npackage pose\n\nimport (\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"testing\"\n)\n\n// Spec pose-federated-proof-reads-each-input-once, R8: what a bundle's bytes\n// say about their scope is parsed once per content; each file keeps its own\n// artifact path, and a changed content is parsed again.\nfunc TestABundleHeaderIsParsedOncePerContent(t *testing.T) {\n\tdir := t.TempDir()\n\twrite := func(name, body string) {\n\t\tt.Helper()\n\t\tif err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(dir, name), []byte(body), 0o600); err != nil {\n\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tgood := `{\"payload\":{\"scope\":{\"ref\":\"spec:alpha\"}}}`\n\twrite(\"rvb-1.json\", good)\n\twrite(\"rvb-2.json\", good)\n\tfiles := NativeFiles()\n\tfirst := readReviewBundleHeader(files, dir, \"rvb-1.json\")\n\tsecond := readReviewBundleHeader(files, dir, \"rvb-2.json\")\n\tif first.owner != \"spec:alpha\" || second.owner != \"spec:alpha\" || first.loss != nil || second.loss != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"owners %q %q losses %v %v\", first.owner, second.owner, first.loss, second.loss)\n\t}\n\twrite(\"rvb-1.json\", `{\"payload\":{\"scope\":{\"ref\":\"spec:beta\"}}}`)\n\tif changed := readReviewBundleHeader(files, dir, \"rvb-1.json\"); changed.owner != \"spec:beta\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a changed content kept the old scope: %q\", changed.owner)\n\t}\n\tbad := `{\"payload\":{\"scope\":{\"ref\":\"spec:alpha\"}},\"payload\":{\"scope\":{\"ref\":\"spec:beta\"}}}`\n\twrite(\"rvb-3.json\", bad)\n\twrite(\"rvb-4.json\", bad)\n\ta := readReviewBundleHeader(files, dir, \"rvb-3.json\")\n\tb := readReviewBundleHeader(files, dir, \"rvb-4.json\")\n\tif a.loss == nil || b.loss == nil || a.loss.Kind != b.loss.Kind || a.loss.Detail != b.loss.Detail {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the loss was not kept whole: %+v %+v\", a.loss, b.loss)\n\t}\n\tif a.loss.Artifact != \".pose/review-bundles/rvb-3.json\" || b.loss.Artifact != \".pose/review-bundles/rvb-4.json\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a kept loss named another file: %q %q\", a.loss.Artifact, b.loss.Artifact)\n\t}\n}\n# Rule: Backend Go\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide for HTTP handlers, domain services, persistence, concurrency, and integrations between Go services.\n\n## Required patterns\n\n- Handle and propagate errors with enough context for diagnosis.\n- Validate handler input and return HTTP status codes consistent with the contract.\n- Keep business rules in service or domain layers, not in transport code.\n- Honor `context.Context` for timeouts and cancellation.\n- Put data access behind clear interfaces that support testing.\n- Use structured logs without exposing sensitive data.\n\n## Blocking anti-patterns\n\n- Ignoring error returns, including `_` for critical errors.\n- Using `panic` in ordinary request flows instead of controlled error handling.\n- Coupling handlers directly to concrete repository implementations without an abstraction.\n- Running unbounded or unpaginated queries in potentially large endpoints.\n- Introducing concurrency without adequate synchronization or race protection.\n\n## Minimum checks\n\n- Run `go test ./...` in the affected scope.\n- Run `go test -race ./...` when concurrency changes.\n- Run `go vet ./...` without blocking findings.\n- Run the applicable Go lint check, such as `golangci-lint`, without critical errors.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Apply the most restrictive security, contract, and operational rule when domain rules conflict.\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence and explicit risk mitigation when speed conflicts with control.\n- Record the precedence decision and objective rationale in the review.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\n# Rule: Documentation Style\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide for process documentation, rules, specs, workflows, and operational instructions.\n\n## Required conventions\n\n- Write instructions in the imperative mood and begin them with action verbs.\n- Keep bullets short and limited to one idea.\n- Avoid duplicating sections with the same purpose across files.\n- Link to the single source of truth instead of copying its content.\n- Use `check`, `spec`, and `workflow` consistently.\n- State each instruction's scope explicitly to reduce ambiguity.\n\n## Examples: good and bad\n\n### Redundancy\n\n- **Good:** \"Update review criteria in `.pose/workflows/review.md` and reference that workflow from the root AGENTS file.\"\n- **Bad:** \"Repeat review criteria in AGENTS, the workflow, and every related spec.\"\n\n### Ambiguous reference\n\n- **Good:** \"Run the lint check described by the review workflow.\"\n- **Bad:** \"Run that standard validation before pushing.\"\n\n## Quick editorial checklist\n\n- Language is imperative and direct.\n- Bullets are short and non-overlapping.\n- Files do not duplicate sections.\n- `check`, `spec`, and `workflow` are used consistently.\n- References point to explicit files or paths.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Apply the most restrictive security, contract, and operational rule when domain rules conflict.\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence and explicit risk mitigation when speed conflicts with control.\n- Record the precedence decision and objective rationale in the review.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '640,900p' pose-mcp/internal/pose/release_manifest_integrity.go; rg -n 'func materialSetDigestAt|func repositoryIsShallow|type SourceLoss|func attributeLoss|func isGitRevision|currentMaterial|releaseSnapshots|releaseTagObjects|reviewBundleHeaderParses' pose-mcp/internal/cli/material* pose-mcp/internal/pose/*.go; cat pose-mcp/go.mod; git rev-parse HEAD\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '640,900p' pose-mcp/internal/pose/release_manifest_integrity.go; rg -n 'func materialSetDigestAt|func repositoryIsShallow|type SourceLoss|func attributeLoss|func isGitRevision|currentMaterial|releaseSnapshots|releaseTagObjects|reviewBundleHeaderParses' pose-mcp/internal/cli/material* pose-mcp/internal/pose/*.go; cat pose-mcp/go.mod; git rev-parse HEAD\"","aggregated_output":"\t\t// that holds no manifest. A snapshot with gaps may record a failing\n\t\t// git read, and is read again (review round 1, F2).\n\t\tkeep := merr == nil && commit != \"\" && (err == nil && snap.Intact() || errors.Is(err, ErrReleaseSnapshotMissing))\n\t\treturn releaseSnapshotEntry{encoded: encoded, err: err}, keep\n\t})\n\tif fresh != nil {\n\t\treturn *fresh, entry.err\n\t}\n\tvar snap ReleaseSnapshot\n\tif err := json.Unmarshal(entry.encoded, &snap); err != nil {\n\t\treturn readReleaseSnapshotAtCommitUncached(root, tag, commit, annotated)\n\t}\n\treturn snap, entry.err\n}\n\nfunc readReleaseSnapshotAtCommitUncached(root, tag, commit string, annotated bool) (ReleaseSnapshot, error) {\n\tif commit == \"\" {\n\t\treturn ReleaseSnapshot{Tag: tag}, fmt.Errorf(\"pose: release tag %s has no captured commit\", tag)\n\t}\n\tsnap := ReleaseSnapshot{Tag: tag, Commit: commit, Annotated: annotated}\n\tmanifestPath := \".pose/releases/\" + tag + \"/manifest.json\"\n\traw, err := gitBlobAt(root, commit, manifestPath)\n\tif errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) {\n\t\treturn snap, fmt.Errorf(\"%w: %s has no %s at %s\", ErrReleaseSnapshotMissing, tag, manifestPath, commit[:12])\n\t}\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn snap, err\n\t}\n\tmanifest, issues := ValidateReleaseManifestBytes(raw, tag)\n\tsnap.Manifest, snap.Issues = manifest, issues\n\tsnap.ManifestDigest = ReleaseDigest(string(raw))\n\tif manifest == nil || manifest.SchemaVersion != ReleaseManifestSchemaVersion {\n\t\treturn snap, nil\n\t}\n\tgap := func(format string, args ...any) { snap.Gaps = append(snap.Gaps, fmt.Sprintf(format, args...)) }\n\t// The policies the cut was made under: the release policy and the changelog policy of the tagged\n\t// tree, held to their own contracts. A policy of today is never applied to a cut of yesterday.\n\tpolicy, policyPath, perr := releasePolicyAt(root, commit)\n\tpolicyKnown := false\n\tswitch {\n\tcase errors.Is(perr, os.ErrNotExist):\n\t\tgap(\"the tagged tree holds no release policy, so the manifest's policy digest cannot be tied to the policy of the cut\")\n\tcase perr != nil:\n\t\tgap(\"the release policy of the tagged tree (%s) could not be read: %v\", policyPath, perr)\n\tdefault:\n\t\tif verr := ValidateReleasePolicy(policy); verr != nil {\n\t\t\tgap(\"the release policy of the tagged tree (%s) does not satisfy its contract: %v\", policyPath, verr)\n\t\t} else if ReleaseDigest(policy) != manifest.PolicyDigest {\n\t\t\tgap(\"the manifest's policy digest is not the digest of the release policy of the tagged tree (%s)\", policyPath)\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tpolicyKnown = true\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tchangelog, clErr := changelogPolicyAt(root, commit)\n\tif clErr != nil {\n\t\tgap(\"the changelog policy of the tagged tree could not be read: %v\", clErr)\n\t}\n\t// Frozen fragments: each is a blob of the tagged tree, with the identity, category,\n\t// breaking flag, references and body the manifest repeats, at a path nothing else uses.\n\tfrozen := map[string]string{}\n\tspecs := map[string]bool{}\n\tfor _, f := range manifest.Fragments {\n\t\tif other, dup := frozen[f.Path]; dup {\n\t\t\tgap(\"fragments %s and %s share the file %s\", other, f.Spec, f.Path)\n\t\t}\n\t\tfrozen[f.Path] = f.Spec\n\t\tif specs[f.Spec] {\n\t\t\tgap(\"fragment spec %s is frozen more than once\", f.Spec)\n\t\t}\n\t\tspecs[f.Spec] = true\n\t\tif clErr == nil && !changelog.ValidCategories()[f.Category] {\n\t\t\tgap(\"fragment %s has category %q, which the changelog policy of the tagged tree does not accept\", f.Spec, f.Category)\n\t\t}\n\t\tblob, ferr := gitBlobAt(root, commit, \".pose/changelogs/\"+tag+\"/\"+f.Path)\n\t\tswitch {\n\t\tcase errors.Is(ferr, os.ErrNotExist):\n\t\t\tgap(\"fragment %s is missing from the tagged tree\", f.Spec)\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\tcase ferr != nil:\n\t\t\tgap(\"fragment %s could not be read from the tagged tree: %v\", f.Spec, ferr)\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\tcase ReleaseDigest(string(blob)) != f.Digest:\n\t\t\tgap(\"fragment %s differs from the digest the manifest froze\", f.Spec)\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tfm, body := SplitFrontmatter(string(blob))\n\t\tbody = strings.TrimSpace(stripHTMLComments(body))\n\t\tswitch {\n\t\tcase strings.TrimSpace(fm[\"spec\"]) != f.Spec:\n\t\t\tgap(\"fragment %s names spec %q in the tagged tree\", f.Spec, strings.TrimSpace(fm[\"spec\"]))\n\t\tcase strings.TrimSpace(fm[\"category\"]) == \"\" || strings.TrimSpace(fm[\"category\"]) != f.Category:\n\t\t\tgap(\"fragment %s has category %q in the tagged tree, the manifest says %q\", f.Spec, strings.TrimSpace(fm[\"category\"]), f.Category)\n\t\tcase (fm[\"breaking\"] == \"true\") != f.Breaking:\n\t\t\tgap(\"fragment %s differs from the manifest in its breaking flag\", f.Spec)\n\t\tcase fm[\"refs\"] != f.Refs:\n\t\t\tgap(\"fragment %s differs from the manifest in its references\", f.Spec)\n\t\tcase body == \"\":\n\t\t\tgap(\"fragment %s has no body in the tagged tree\", f.Spec)\n\t\tcase body != f.Body:\n\t\t\tgap(\"fragment %s differs from the manifest in its body\", f.Spec)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tnames, exists, lerr := gitTreeNames(root, commit, \".pose/changelogs/\"+tag)\n\tswitch {\n\tcase lerr != nil:\n\t\tgap(\"the fragments of the tagged tree could not be listed: %v\", lerr)\n\tcase !exists && len(manifest.Fragments) > 0:\n\t\tgap(\"the tagged tree holds no fragment directory, the manifest freezes %d\", len(manifest.Fragments))\n\tdefault:\n\t\tsort.Strings(names)\n\t\tfor _, name := range names {\n\t\t\tif _, ok := frozen[name]; !ok {\n\t\t\t\tgap(\"the tagged tree holds fragment %s that the manifest does not freeze\", name)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\t// Notes: the blob is the rendering of the frozen fragments under the changelog policy of the\n\t// cut, not whatever text its digest was recomputed for.\n\tnotes, err := gitBlobAt(root, commit, \".pose/changelogs/\"+tag+\".md\")\n\tswitch {\n\tcase errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist):\n\t\tgap(\"canonical notes are missing from the tagged tree\")\n\tcase err != nil:\n\t\tgap(\"canonical notes could not be read from the tagged tree: %v\", err)\n\tcase ReleaseDigest(string(notes)) != manifest.NotesDigest:\n\t\tgap(\"canonical notes digest differs from the manifest in the tagged tree\")\n\tcase clErr == nil:\n\t\tif ok, limit := NotesAreTheRendering(string(notes), manifest.Version, manifest.Fragments, changelog); !ok {\n\t\t\tgap(\"the canonical notes of the tagged tree are not the rendering of the fragments the manifest froze\")\n\t\t} else if limit != \"\" {\n\t\t\tsnap.Limits = append(snap.Limits, limit)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\t// Version evidence: the file of the tagged tree that holds the version, read the way the\n\t// policy of the cut says to read it.\n\tif g, limit := snapshotVersionEvidence(root, commit, manifest, policy, policyKnown); g != \"\" {\n\t\tsnap.Gaps = append(snap.Gaps, g)\n\t} else if limit != \"\" {\n\t\tsnap.Limits = append(snap.Limits, limit)\n\t}\n\treturn snap, nil\n}\n\nfunc changelogPolicyAt(root, commit string) (ChangelogPolicy, error) {\n\traw, err := gitBlobAt(root, commit, \".pose/policy/changelog.json\")\n\tswitch {\n\tcase errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist):\n\t\treturn ChangelogPolicy{SchemaVersion: 1, Categories: DefaultChangelogCategories()}, nil\n\tcase err != nil:\n\t\treturn ChangelogPolicy{}, err\n\t}\n\treturn ParseChangelogPolicy(raw)\n}\n\n// snapshotVersionEvidence re-verifies the version a manifest recorded against the file of the\n// tagged tree that holds it, read the way the release policy of that tree says to read it. The\n// evidence must name the source and the kind the policy names (or the engine's own version file\n// when the policy names none); the file must be there; a text or JSON source is read and must say\n// the version of the release. What cannot be recomputed is a limit, and only that.\nfunc snapshotVersionEvidence(root, commit string, manifest *ReleaseManifest, policy ReleasePolicy, policyKnown bool) (gap, limit string) {\n\tsource, kind, value := manifest.VersionEvidence[\"source\"], manifest.VersionEvidence[\"kind\"], manifest.VersionEvidence[\"value\"]\n\tif source == \"\" {\n\t\treturn \"the manifest records no version source\", \"\"\n\t}\n\tif !policyKnown {\n\t\treturn \"\", \"the version evidence was not tied to the release policy of the cut, which could not be verified\"\n\t}\n\tif policy.VersionSource != nil {\n\t\tif filepath.ToSlash(filepath.Clean(policy.VersionSource.Path)) != source || policy.VersionSource.Kind != kind {\n\t\t\treturn \"the version evidence names a source or kind other than the version_source of the release policy of the tagged tree\", \"\"\n\t\t}\n\t} else if source != EngineVersionFile || kind != \"\" {\n\t\treturn \"the release policy of the tagged tree declares no version_source, and the version evidence names a source other than the engine's own version file\", \"\"\n\t}\n\traw, err := gitBlobAt(root, commit, source)\n\tif errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) {\n\t\treturn \"version source \" + source + \" is missing from the tagged tree\", \"\"\n\t}\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"version source \" + source + \" could not be read from the tagged tree: \" + err.Error(), \"\"\n\t}\n\tif policy.VersionSource == nil {\n\t\t// The engine's own version file is a Go source that holds the number in code.\n\t\treturn \"\", \"the version evidence is the engine's own version file: it exists in the tagged tree and its value was not re-read\"\n\t}\n\tgot, err := parseReleaseVersionSource(raw, *policy.VersionSource)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"version source \" + source + \" in the tagged tree is not readable: \" + err.Error(), \"\"\n\t}\n\tif got != value || got != manifest.Version {\n\t\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"the tagged tree's version source says %s, the manifest recorded %s for %s\", got, value, manifest.Version), \"\"\n\t}\n\treturn \"\", \"\"\n}\n\n// gitCommandError keeps the exit status of a failed git command, so a caller can tell \"there is\n// no such thing\" (an exit status Git documents) from \"Git could not answer\".\ntype gitCommandError struct {\n\tOp     string\n\tStderr string\n\tErr    error\n}\n\nfunc (e *gitCommandError) Error() string {\n\treturn \"pose: git \" + e.Op + \": \" + strings.TrimSpace(e.Stderr+\" \"+e.Err.Error())\n}\nfunc (e *gitCommandError) Unwrap() error { return e.Err }\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_operation_view.go:38:\tparsed := reviewBundleHeaderParses.get(string(result.digest[:]), func() (reviewBundleHeaderParse, bool) {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_operation_view.go:74:// reviewBundleHeaderParses keeps, per content digest, the scope a bundle\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_operation_view.go:76:var reviewBundleHeaderParses = newKeptCache[reviewBundleHeaderParse](50000)\npose-mcp/internal/pose/release_manifest_integrity.go:382:\tpeeled := releaseTagObjects.get(filepath.Clean(root)+\"\\x00\"+object, func() (releaseTagObject, bool) {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/release_manifest_integrity.go:428:// releaseTagObjects keeps, per repository and tag object, the commit the\npose-mcp/internal/pose/release_manifest_integrity.go:431:var releaseTagObjects = newKeptCache[releaseTagObject](10000)\npose-mcp/internal/pose/release_manifest_integrity.go:618:// releaseSnapshots holds, per process, the snapshot read for a tag at a\npose-mcp/internal/pose/release_manifest_integrity.go:625:var releaseSnapshots = newKeptCache[releaseSnapshotEntry](10000)\npose-mcp/internal/pose/release_manifest_integrity.go:635:\tentry := releaseSnapshots.get(key, func() (releaseSnapshotEntry, bool) {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/material_bind